-- Passage rg9-comparing-texts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a103a213-ac53-5802-b32a-a4cabb12163f', 'rg9-comparing-texts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Many Hands$q$, $q$Text 1: The Bridge of Seven Stones (an invented folk tale)

Long ago, two villages stood on either side of a cold, fast stream. Each spring the water rose, and no one could cross to trade or visit. The elders of both villages met on the banks and shouted across the roar. "No single family can carry enough stone to build a bridge," one elder called. "Then let every family carry one," answered the other.

So each household brought a single stone, and the builders set them one beside another. Some stones were round, some flat, some cracked along one edge. Alone, none of them could hold back a puddle. Together they made an arch that carried carts for a hundred years. Children in both villages learned to point at the bridge and name the stone their great-grandparents had given.

Text 2: Before the Concert (a poem that retells the tale)

The oboe gives one steady note,
a single stone set on the bank.
The violins lean in to match it,
then the cellos, rank by rank.

No one of us could fill this hall.
My viola part is thin and plain,
four measures of the same low line,
then rest, then the same line again.

But listen when the music starts:
the flutes are standing on my sound,
the trumpets cross on what we built
from every player gathered round.

And when the final chord is done,
I'll hear my thread inside the whole,
the way a child can find one stone
and know her family paid the toll.$q$, 'draft')
