-- Passage rg9-comparing-texts-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('849e954b-34f1-59da-bde2-c7abf33fda03', 'rg9-comparing-texts-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Blank Corner$q$, $q$Text 1: The Mapmaker's Blank Corner (an invented legend)

In a city of narrow streets lived Tamsin Vell, the finest mapmaker of her age. Kings and sailors paid her to draw coasts, rivers and mountain passes. When she finished her greatest map, the apprentices gasped, because the northeast corner was empty except for a small drawing of an open door. "Did you run out of time?" one asked. Tamsin shook her head. "I ran out of knowing," she said. "That corner belongs to whoever goes there next."

For years, travelers pointed to the blank corner and laughed. Then a young surveyor crossed the eastern hills and drew a lake there, writing her own name beside it. Others followed. The map grew richer with every hand, and Tamsin's open door was never painted over.

Text 2: Observing Log, Page 41 (a story that retells the legend)

The astronomy club's logbook lived in a metal box in the school's rooftop dome. Senior members had filled forty pages with careful records of comets, eclipses and star brightness. Page 41 was blank except for one line in faded ink: "Star HV-118 changes. Somebody please find out why."

Hiroshi Banda read that line on his first night in the dome, three years after the student who wrote it had graduated. All winter he measured the star's brightness through the club's small telescope. The numbers rose and fell on a pattern of almost exactly nine days, and his advisor helped him report it to an online database that professional astronomers check.

On his last night before graduation, Hiroshi copied his chart onto page 41. Then he turned to page 42 and wrote a new line at the top: "The pattern is not perfect. Somebody please find out why."$q$, 'draft')
