-- Passage rg9-comparing-texts-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('3b455230-0936-58e9-b7bb-34dc54865426', 'rg9-comparing-texts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-comparing-texts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Wheel That Turned Itself$q$, $q$Text 1: The Miller's Clever Wheel (an invented folk tale)

A miller named Brannoch grew tired of tending his mill. So he built a wheel with gears that opened the sluice, fed the grain and swept the floor all by themselves. For ten years the mill ran without him, and Brannoch spent his days fishing and telling visitors how clever he was.

One harvest a wooden gear split in two. The wheel stopped, and grain piled up in the yard. Brannoch climbed inside the machine and stared at the gears, but he had forgotten how each one connected to the next. His neighbor, an old woman who still ground her flour by hand, walked over with her small stone quern. "Machines are fine servants," she said, "but a poor memory." The miller ground flour beside her for a week while he slowly relearned his own mill.

Text 2: Print Night (a story that retells the tale)

Kenji Adeyemi had written a script that turned the history club's articles into a finished newsletter with one click. It fixed the fonts, placed the photos and numbered the pages. For two years, nobody in the club touched the layout by hand.

At nine o'clock on the night before the spring issue was due at the printer, the script stopped working after a software update. Kenji opened the code, but he had written it so long ago that he no longer understood half of it. The club members looked at one another. None of them knew how to set margins or line up columns without the script.

Then Ms. Ostrova, the club's advisor, pulled a ruler and a stack of old printed issues from a drawer. "We did this by hand for years," she said. Until midnight, the club laid out the pages together, measuring every column. The issue went out on time, and Kenji added a page of plain instructions to the script's folder.$q$, 'draft')
